<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hyksos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hyksos were a people of West Asian origin who ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>. They introduced or popularised technologies such as the horse-drawn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>. Their expulsion by Theban rulers led to the foundation of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and its militarised expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hyksos.jpg" alt="Hyksos" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NebMaatRa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibscha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In modern Egyptology the name Hyksos, from the Egyptian ḥqꜣ-ḫꜣswt, 'rulers of foreign lands', refers to the kings of the Fifteenth Dynasty (c. 1650 – c. 1550 BCE), rather than to a people, although the later Greek writers used it ethnically. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>, a Greco-Egyptian priest of the 3rd century BCE, whose work survives only in quotation, portrayed them as invaders who burnt cities and razed temples; modern Egyptology questions this account.</p><p>Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Tell el-Dab'a</a>, the site of their capital Avaris, since 1966 indicate a Levantine origin. Their personal names are West Semitic, and they may be called Canaanites for convenience. Canaanite groups may have settled gradually in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> from the end of the Twelfth Dynasty and seceded from weakening Egyptian control during the Thirteenth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> the Hyksos ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> as far as Cusae, while the Sixteenth and Seventeenth Dynasties coexisted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> names six Hyksos kings who ruled 108 years, though other scholars read the figure as higher; only the last king, Khamudi, is preserved by name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyan">Khyan</a> left objects at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and Hattusha, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> letter found at Avaris shows long-distance diplomacy.</p><p>The war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and the Hyksos is known only from Theban sources, which present it as a war of national liberation, a view scholars no longer hold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamose">Kamose</a>, last king of the Seventeenth Dynasty, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apepi">Apepi</a> a chieftain of Retjenu. Ahmose I finally defeated the Hyksos and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>.</p><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> condemned the Hyksos, and the Nineteenth-Dynasty tale of Apophis and Seqenenre claimed they worshipped no god but Set. About eighty years after their defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> claimed to rebuild temples they had neglected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> connected them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but modern scholars do not accept that the biblical story can be shown by historical methods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their rulers took the full Egyptian royal titulary and employed Egyptian scribes, but they also used Near Eastern forms of administration, including a chancellor at the head of the government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apepi">Apepi</a> had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</a> copied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> had temples in both Egyptian and Levantine style, and the Hyksos worshipped the Canaanite storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, identified with the Egyptian Set. They buried their dead within settlements and also buried horses and other equids.</p><p>The Hyksos have been credited with the horse, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, the sickle sword and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>, but this is disputed. No evidence shows that they possessed chariots, which first appear in Egyptian hands in the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-son-of-ebana">Ahmose, son of Ebana</a>, at the end of their rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> was the commercial capital of the Delta, trading mainly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hyksos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Kingdom began with the expulsion of the <b>Hyksos</b>, an Asiatic dynasty that had controlled Lower Egypt for over a century during the Second Intermediate Period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus, one of the main sources for Egyptian mathematics, was copied under the <b>Hyksos</b> from an original written in the reign of Amenemhat III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Circumstances favoured Mitanni's rise: the Hittite kingdom had fallen into turmoil after the murder of Mursili I, and Egypt was only beginning to reassert itself in the Levant after the <b>Hyksos</b> period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Hyksos</b> period, when chariot-mounted maryannu ruled Egypt from Avaris, Baal was identified with the Egyptian god Set and was thereafter shown wearing the crown of Lower Egypt, while Asherah, Astarte and Anat were given Hathor-like wigs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>When Egyptian power weakened in the Second Intermediate Period, Kerma reached its height, absorbed the kingdom of Sai and allied with the <b>Hyksos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian stone vessels and scarabs reached Crete, and a stone lid inscribed with the name of the <b>Hyksos</b> king Khyan was found at Knossos.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
